WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.277 --> 00:00:01.339
- I mean, now that we're here,

00:00:01.339 --> 00:00:02.907
I'm thinking about how much
water I've gone through

00:00:02.907 --> 00:00:04.161
just today.

00:00:04.161 --> 00:00:07.578
This is gonna be a really hard challenge.

00:00:11.326 --> 00:00:12.913
So, I grew up pretty poor.

00:00:12.913 --> 00:00:15.317
I think I have some idea of what it's like

00:00:15.317 --> 00:00:19.365
to not have full access
to things that I need.

00:00:19.365 --> 00:00:21.804
Water is like a whole other level though.

00:00:21.804 --> 00:00:24.172
- I'm a little nervous to
actually have to go through that.

00:00:24.172 --> 00:00:26.999
The only time I've really
not had running water

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:29.719
is when I would go to India as a kid.

00:00:29.719 --> 00:00:31.555
We'd also have to have boiled water

00:00:31.555 --> 00:00:32.915
because the actual tap water,

00:00:32.915 --> 00:00:34.366
any other running water source,

00:00:34.366 --> 00:00:35.655
would affect us pretty badly,

00:00:35.655 --> 00:00:36.659
we'd get really sick.

00:00:36.659 --> 00:00:38.841
And it's weird because it's not even

00:00:38.841 --> 00:00:42.507
to the actual degree that people
in Flint are going through.

00:00:42.507 --> 00:00:45.191
- Although I'm obviously
really down to do this,

00:00:45.191 --> 00:00:48.647
part of me just feels like
a spoiled little shit still,

00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:51.096
to be honest, because what
I'm about to go through

00:00:51.096 --> 00:00:53.707
is still just a tiny fraction of what

00:00:53.707 --> 00:00:56.007
so many people around the
world go through every day.

00:00:56.007 --> 00:00:57.383
- My name is Joel Epstein.

00:00:57.383 --> 00:00:59.707
I'm a Los Angeles based
communication strategist

00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:00.769
and also a lawyer.

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:02.154
And I've written extensively on

00:01:02.154 --> 00:01:03.683
crimes against the environment

00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:06.095
and efforts to clean up
the environment nationally.

00:01:06.095 --> 00:01:09.839
Some of our cities need
to upgrade their systems,

00:01:09.839 --> 00:01:11.637
but there's a reluctance
on the part of the current

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:13.979
constituency, the
residents and politicians

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:16.974
to have the vision to make the investment

00:01:16.974 --> 00:01:18.159
that's needed to be made.

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:20.297
Flint, Michigan is the classic example.

00:01:20.297 --> 00:01:21.936
When it was discovered that there were

00:01:21.936 --> 00:01:23.898
very high levels of lead in the water,

00:01:23.898 --> 00:01:25.833
the residents of Flint are still drinking

00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:28.585
and showering using bottled water.

00:01:28.585 --> 00:01:31.223
Tremendous expense, tremendous
environmental problem,

00:01:31.223 --> 00:01:33.846
and it's really just
completely unacceptable.

00:01:33.846 --> 00:01:34.955
Water is a right.

00:01:34.955 --> 00:01:38.862
Water is something that a
citizen should be able to expect

00:01:38.862 --> 00:01:40.041
from their government.

00:01:40.041 --> 00:01:42.188
- So, day one, we're
gonna use a pump nozzle.

00:01:42.188 --> 00:01:44.305
- Day two, I'm gonna be
showering at the gym I go to.

00:01:44.305 --> 00:01:47.042
- Day three, in and out of
the shower with mouth closed.

00:01:47.042 --> 00:01:48.879
- [Shane] Four, I'm gonna
use some baby wipes.

00:01:48.879 --> 00:01:52.269
- Day five, boiling water and sponging.

00:01:52.269 --> 00:01:54.394
This should keep me going for a few days.

00:01:54.394 --> 00:01:56.685
The pump was a little tricky to use,

00:01:56.685 --> 00:01:59.307
but I was lucky enough to
be able to afford that pump.

00:01:59.307 --> 00:02:02.441
- I thought I had figured
out how to assemble it,

00:02:02.441 --> 00:02:05.714
but I didn't because when I
started pouring water into it,

00:02:05.714 --> 00:02:06.961
it went everywhere.

00:02:06.961 --> 00:02:08.046
- I could not, for the life of me,

00:02:08.046 --> 00:02:10.529
figure out how the fuck
to use the pumping spray.

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:12.986
This doesn't feel very self-explanatory.

00:02:12.986 --> 00:02:16.387
I think I might just dump a
bottle of water on my head.

00:02:16.387 --> 00:02:17.669
- Today is day two.

00:02:17.669 --> 00:02:21.736
I have my towel, my mini
soap, and all my clothes

00:02:21.736 --> 00:02:25.961
in my backpack, and today I'm
gonna be showering at the gym.

00:02:25.961 --> 00:02:29.504
The gym was the best because
at least I have access

00:02:29.504 --> 00:02:32.778
to the running water, so I
stayed in there for 15 minutes

00:02:32.778 --> 00:02:34.621
before I felt like I had to leave.

00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:38.029
- I just started doing
the dishes, and I realized

00:02:38.029 --> 00:02:39.762
that I can't actually rinse them

00:02:39.762 --> 00:02:42.345
'cause I can't turn the tap on.

00:02:46.013 --> 00:02:46.867
- Fuck.

00:02:46.867 --> 00:02:48.313
I just left my friend's apartment

00:02:48.313 --> 00:02:50.098
after borrowing her shower,

00:02:50.098 --> 00:02:52.309
and it was fantastic.

00:02:52.309 --> 00:02:54.543
It was also uncomfortable to

00:02:54.543 --> 00:02:56.670
do all the things you do in a bathroom

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.065
which are kinda intimate and private

00:02:58.065 --> 00:02:59.770
in someone else's bathroom.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.297
You just feel naked and afraid.

00:03:02.297 --> 00:03:04.584
- So, I have a packed bag

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:07.523
with my towel and toiletries.

00:03:07.523 --> 00:03:10.339
Today, I'm going to shower at work.

00:03:10.339 --> 00:03:11.732
It's not the first time,

00:03:11.732 --> 00:03:14.319
so this shouldn't be too odd.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.493
But the thought of
having to do it every day

00:03:17.493 --> 00:03:19.031
would be pretty uncomfortable.

00:03:19.031 --> 00:03:22.674
- I don't think I had any
issue asking for help once

00:03:22.674 --> 00:03:23.806
from a friend.

00:03:23.806 --> 00:03:27.286
I could see how that would
take a big emotional toll,

00:03:27.286 --> 00:03:29.867
relying on other people to
provide you running water.

00:03:29.867 --> 00:03:30.868
Yeah, that would suck.

00:03:30.868 --> 00:03:32.285
- I'm so thirsty.

00:03:37.355 --> 00:03:38.407
Damn.

00:03:38.407 --> 00:03:41.157
- Day three, we jumped
in and out of the shower

00:03:41.157 --> 00:03:42.160
really quickly.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.677
Apparently, that's a thing that people do

00:03:43.677 --> 00:03:45.005
in this situation.

00:03:45.005 --> 00:03:48.150
I ended up just cleaning myself
with baby wipes a day early.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:51.670
- I tried to take the water
droplets and rub them on myself

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:54.883
with the towel and scrub
myself with the towel.

00:03:54.883 --> 00:03:57.522
I felt really sticky after
because I still felt the need

00:03:57.522 --> 00:03:59.820
to moisturize because
I have really dry skin.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.626
- It's time for operation baby wipes.

00:04:02.626 --> 00:04:04.992
- Day four was probably the easiest day

00:04:04.992 --> 00:04:07.502
to clean myself and the
least emotionally tolling.

00:04:07.502 --> 00:04:09.200
Baby wipes are great.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.633
I might do that more often.

00:04:10.633 --> 00:04:14.083
- Wiped myself down with
scented baby wipes today.

00:04:14.083 --> 00:04:15.574
Not the business.

00:04:15.574 --> 00:04:16.897
They smell great.

00:04:16.897 --> 00:04:18.900
They don't do anything for you.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.032
I probably used up half the package.

00:04:21.032 --> 00:04:24.210
I was just kind of like
scrubbing under my arms.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.135
- I'm not gonna wipe this everywhere,

00:04:26.135 --> 00:04:27.292
if you know what I mean.

00:04:27.292 --> 00:04:30.758
The thing that gets me
the most is underarms.

00:04:30.758 --> 00:04:33.961
After having to do this
only after a couple days,

00:04:33.961 --> 00:04:37.711
this still feels like
I'm lucky to have this.

00:04:38.619 --> 00:04:41.600
Gonna smell like a baby all day.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.368
- I did not use paper plates at all,

00:04:43.368 --> 00:04:46.833
but I also ended up putting
off washing my dishes

00:04:46.833 --> 00:04:48.991
because it's so fucking hard

00:04:48.991 --> 00:04:50.228
when you don't have running water.

00:04:50.228 --> 00:04:53.486
- Really the hardest part
was just the emotional strain

00:04:53.486 --> 00:04:55.706
of knowing that this
would be over in a week

00:04:55.706 --> 00:04:57.540
and that there are hundreds
of thousands of people

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.565
in the world who have to
live this every single day.

00:05:00.565 --> 00:05:01.789
- I became really, really aware

00:05:01.789 --> 00:05:03.567
of how much water I was using.

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:05.581
I would never let water run down the drain

00:05:05.581 --> 00:05:06.667
without thinking to myself,

00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:08.921
okay is there anything else
I can rinse off with this?

00:05:08.921 --> 00:05:10.309
- I notice when it's raining

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:12.555
there's these repeat offender homes

00:05:12.555 --> 00:05:14.635
and they all have their sprinklers running

00:05:14.635 --> 00:05:15.950
while it's raining.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.551
That's water someone else in Flint

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:19.687
or anywhere else in the world

00:05:19.687 --> 00:05:20.897
where they don't get access to water

00:05:20.897 --> 00:05:22.865
could be using but you're just wasting it.

00:05:22.865 --> 00:05:24.745
I feel like we don't fully understand that

00:05:24.745 --> 00:05:27.933
until you kind of cut yourself
off from what you have.

00:05:27.933 --> 00:05:30.162
You just don't realize how
bad it is for other people.

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:31.821
I wasn't able to shower in the morning,

00:05:31.821 --> 00:05:34.747
so I showered at night
with the boiled water

00:05:34.747 --> 00:05:35.924
'cause it took so long to cool

00:05:35.924 --> 00:05:38.904
and I had to boil so much
of it to actually use it.

00:05:38.904 --> 00:05:41.839
It was definitely an exercise in patience.

00:05:41.839 --> 00:05:44.965
- But hair, goddamn, I don't
know how you can do any of this

00:05:44.965 --> 00:05:46.593
with long hair.

00:05:46.593 --> 00:05:49.379
Even short hair, I haven't
washed my hair at all

00:05:49.379 --> 00:05:50.563
in the past few days.

00:05:50.563 --> 00:05:53.141
I've lived in really poor
areas of the Middle East

00:05:53.141 --> 00:05:55.716
where we didn't have running water,

00:05:55.716 --> 00:05:57.930
or when we did, it wasn't clean.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:05:59.878
That was only for a few months at a time.

00:05:59.878 --> 00:06:01.437
But to live that every day,

00:06:01.437 --> 00:06:04.757
to have a baby and to
not be able to bath it

00:06:04.757 --> 00:06:06.942
or long hair that you need to wash

00:06:06.942 --> 00:06:10.841
and it can't get in your eyes
or mouth, every single day.

00:06:10.841 --> 00:06:13.586
- It was easier than I
expected in some ways

00:06:13.586 --> 00:06:17.390
and also surprised me at how
hard it was in other ways.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:19.671
- I was able to connect a little
bit more to that situation

00:06:19.671 --> 00:06:20.796
because you can read an article

00:06:20.796 --> 00:06:22.381
but it's not the same as going through it.

00:06:22.381 --> 00:06:25.097
- We can recreate not having running water

00:06:25.097 --> 00:06:27.100
but we can't recreate
the emotional experience

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.294
of being forced to not have running water

00:06:29.294 --> 00:06:31.402
and not knowing when you're
gonna get running water back.

00:06:31.402 --> 00:06:33.566
What I got out of this was it taught me

00:06:33.566 --> 00:06:36.308
how much of a necessity running water is

00:06:36.308 --> 00:06:38.681
and that it's not really
something that can

00:06:38.681 --> 00:06:40.997
ever be up for debate.

00:06:40.997 --> 00:06:42.317
- The biggest thing about this video

00:06:42.317 --> 00:06:46.091
is that this could easily come
across as poverty tourism.

00:06:46.091 --> 00:06:48.866
I don't want this to
be seen as some kind of

00:06:48.866 --> 00:06:52.117
voyeuristic entertainment
from other people's suffering.

00:06:52.117 --> 00:06:54.215
It's more just to get the word out there

00:06:54.215 --> 00:06:57.217
and to hopefully showcase that

00:06:57.217 --> 00:06:59.335
it's ridiculous that this is happening

00:06:59.335 --> 00:07:00.760
in developed countries

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.227
but it's ridiculous that
it's happening full stop.

00:07:03.227 --> 00:07:04.182
- The least I can do,

00:07:04.182 --> 00:07:05.413
especially after doing this experiment,

00:07:05.413 --> 00:07:08.025
is make a little noise about it.

00:07:08.025 --> 00:07:10.542
- If we all kind of band
together and make it a big deal,

00:07:10.542 --> 00:07:12.344
maybe something will happen faster

00:07:12.344 --> 00:07:14.075
because there will be
more attention on it.

00:07:14.075 --> 00:07:15.871
- There is a lot of information out there

00:07:15.871 --> 00:07:18.114
if you're willing to look for it.

00:07:18.114 --> 00:07:20.163
EPA is a good place to start.

00:07:20.163 --> 00:07:21.955
The Natural Resources Defense Council

00:07:21.955 --> 00:07:23.464
is another excellent organization

00:07:23.464 --> 00:07:25.555
that's very focused on this.

00:07:25.555 --> 00:07:27.559
Earthjustice, places like that.

00:07:27.559 --> 00:07:31.355
- Being mad or sad about any problem

00:07:31.355 --> 00:07:32.720
is obviously never enough.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.219
It's actually about standing
up and doing something

00:07:35.219 --> 00:07:39.386
and hopefully this video
gets that message across.

